redirectfixup is a Python utility designed to clean up and canonicalize HTML files by resolving 301 Moved Permanently redirects and flattening absolute URLs into local relative paths. 

It was designed for archiving a set of legacy sites from an esoteric CMS to a set of flat files. Some of the content from these sites had been split up over a multitude of subdomains for reasons relating to serving architecture and administrator
convenience, and at archive time we wanted to unwind this where possible. Our rough process for archiving these sites was to use curl to scrape many pages down to a set of files, use apache mod_rewrite rules to make the static pages
work, and then use this tool to replace the redirects and canonicalize all links/references to be site-local.

Core Features

  Automatic 301 Resolution: Performs network HEAD requests to find where links have moved and updates the HTML to point to the new destination.

  URL Canonicalization: Converts absolute URLs (e.g., http://dev.oursite.org/path/) into clean, relative paths (e.g., /path/).

  Cross-domain management: configuration can express that many domains should be regarded as equivalent (e.g. for ServerAlias) and can even
  support some domains which should only-sometimes be rewritten as local (i.e. 301 redirects -> rewrite the HTML, 302 redirects -> leave HTML alone)

  Dry Run Support: Test your changes on single files before performing a bulk update on an entire directory.

Installation

Ensure you have Python 3.8+ installed. The script relies on requests and beautifulsoup4.

```
pip install requests beautiful
```

Configuration (sites.json)

The behavior of the script is driven by a sites.json file. This file defines the target directories and the logic for how different domains are handled.

Configuration Keys

Key	Description
target_directory	The local folder containing the .html files you want to process.
default_base_domain	The "virtual" root URL used to resolve relative paths in your HTML.
site_config	The Lookup Layer. Maps various hostnames to a "lookup base." If a hostname is found here, the script will perform network requests against this base to check for redirects.
perma_site_config	The Rewrite Layer. Determines which hostnames should be converted into local relative paths. If not specified, site_config is reused for this.

The "Mirror" Logic (Advanced)

The distinction between site_config and perma_site_config allows for a very specific cleanup workflow:

 Scenario: You have a primary site (primary.example.org) and a mirror site (maybemirror.example.org). The mirror site mirrors your content, perhaps under maybemirror.example.org/site/primary/ but the mirror site may also be mirroring links from other sites.
 Goal: You want to turn all links to the mirror that truly reference primary into local links, but leave mirror links to other sites (e.g. maybemirror.example.org/site/foreign/) alone.
 Setup:
  Add the mirror hostname to site_config but exclude it from perma_site_config. Point the mirror hostname at a webserver you control.
  Configure your webserver so any request to your content (e.g. maybemirror.example.org/site/primary/.*) is 301 redirected, but other paths to the mirror site just return 302 found.
  Result: When the script runs, it sees the link to the mirror, follows the 301 to the main site, realizes the new destination is in perma_site_config, and rewrites the link to a local relative path. Any links that return a 302 or don't redirect are left as absolute mirror links.


Usage

Basic Command

To process all HTML files for a defined site in sites.json:

```
python redirectixup.py oursite
```

Single File Testing (Dry Run)

Before running a bulk update, test how a single file will be transformed:
```
python redirectixup.py oursite path/to/test.html
```

Advanced Options

Argument	Description
site (positional)	The key name within sites.json to use for configuration.
--regex <pattern>	Only process files whose local path matches this regex.
--exempt <pattern>	Use a regex to skip normalization for specific local link patterns.
--check-redirects	(Default: True) Whether to perform network requests to follow 301 redirects. If false, only rewriting of local URLs (from perma_site_config) to relative URLs will happen.
single_file (optional)	A specific file path to process in isolation.

Example Commands

Filter processing to only a specific subfolder:

```
python redirectixup.py oursite --regex "archives/2023/"
```

Disable redirect checking (only perform path cleaning):

```
python redirectixup.py oursite --no-check-redirects
```


Example sites.json Structure
```
{
  "sites": {
    "oursite": {
      "target_directory": "./oursite",
      "default_base_domain": "http://dev.oursite.example.org",
      "site_config": {
        "oursite.example.org": "http://dev.oursite.example.org",
        "oursite.example.com": "http://dev.oursite.example.org",
        "maybemirror.oursite.example.org": "http://mirrordev.oursite.example.org"
      },
      "perma_site_config": {
        "oursite.example.org": "http://dev.oursite.example.org",
        "oursite.example.com": "http://dev.oursite.example.org"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
